items.body:
Handing Carmen three terrorist heads pays out $20,000 instead of $30,000. One or two heads pay correctly; three loses exactly one head's bounty.

The reporter's own guess — that $20,000 is a per-slot money limit — is **correct**, and the slot is `MAX_MONEY_PER_SLOT`.

## Reproducing

1. Start the quest, meet Carmen, and collect at least three terrorist heads.
2. Give the heads to Carmen and change sector (the payout happens on the sector change).
3. Talk to Carmen in C13 to collect.

| Heads | Owed | Player receives | Lost |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | $10,000 | $10,000 | — |
| 2 | $20,000 | $20,000 | — |
| 3 | $30,000 | **$20,000** | $10,000 |
| 4 | $40,000 | **$20,000** | $20,000 |

One and two heads fit in a single pocket stack, which is why the bug only appears at three.

## Root cause: the bounty is physical money objects, not an amount

The payout is not a transaction. `src/game/Strategic/Strategic_Event_Handler.cc:813-831` credits the **profile** a scalar:

```cpp
if (gMercProfiles[ CARMEN ].bNPCData2 > 0)
{
    if (gMercProfiles[ CARMEN ].uiMoney < 10000) uiAmount = 0;
    else                                        uiAmount = gMercProfiles[ CARMEN ].uiMoney;
    uiAmount += 10000 * gMercProfiles[ CARMEN ].bNPCData2;   // $10k per head
    SetMoneyInSoldierProfile( CARMEN, uiAmount );
    gMercProfiles[ CARMEN ].bNPCData2 = 0;
    ...
}
```

So 3 heads is correctly recorded as $30,000. Nothing is lost here.

The money becomes objects when Carmen is spawned into a sector. `CopyProfileItems` (`src/game/Tactical/Soldier_Create.cc:2059-2069`) splits the scalar into pocket-sized stacks:

```cpp
for (UINT32 money_left = p.uiMoney; money_left > 0;)
{
    INT8 const slot_id = FindEmptySlotWithin(&s, POCK1POS, POCK12POS);
    if (slot_id == NO_SLOT) break;
    UINT32 const slot_limit  = MoneySlotLimit(slot_id);
    UINT32 const slot_amount = std::min(money_left, slot_limit);
    CreateMoney(slot_amount, slot);
    money_left -= slot_amount;
}
```

`MoneySlotLimit` (`src/game/Tactical/Items.cc:325-335`) caps a stack at `MAX_MONEY_PER_SLOT` = **20000** (`src/game/Tactical/Item_Types.h:40`), or 10000 in `POCK5POS` and later. $30,000 therefore becomes **$20,000 in POCK1 + $10,000 in POCK2** — two separate money objects.

## The handover only ever gives the first one

Carmen pays the player as a **gift item**. His NPC record 24 (`078.NPC`, confirmed by decoding `Npcdata.slf`) carries `usGiftItem = 219` (`MONEY`). The gift path in `src/game/TacticalAI/NPC.cc:1810-1813`:

```cpp
INT8 const bInvPos = FindObj( pSoldier, pQuotePtr->usGiftItem );
if (bInvPos == NO_SLOT) throw std::logic_error("NPC.cc: Gift item does not exist in NPC.");
TalkingMenuGiveItem( ubNPC, &(pSoldier->inv[ bInvPos ] ), bInvPos );
```

`FindObj` returns the **first** matching slot only. The player receives POCK1's $20,000; POCK2's $10,000 is left in Carmen's inventory and never handed over. The `throw` on a missing item is an uncaught `std::logic_error` — a crash, not a warning, in a flow with no test coverage.

This affects **any** NPC payout that exceeds one stack, not just Carmen — any `usGiftItem = MONEY` record whose holder carries more than `MAX_MONEY_PER_SLOT` underpays by the remainder.

## Related defect: unsigned underflow in the money-slot merge

The same money path has a latent overflow. `DropObjInSlot` (`src/game/Tactical/Items.cc:2040-2056`):

```cpp
UINT32 uiMoneyMax = MoneySlotLimit( bPos );
if (pInSlot->uiMoneyAmount + pObj->uiMoneyAmount > uiMoneyMax)
{
    pObj->uiMoneyAmount -= (uiMoneyMax - pInSlot->uiMoneyAmount);
    pInSlot->uiMoneyAmount = uiMoneyMax;
}
```

If `pInSlot->uiMoneyAmount` already **exceeds** `uiMoneyMax`, the subtraction underflows and the leftover stack is corrupted rather than dropped. That state is reachable because `PlaceObjectInSoldierProfile` (`src/game/Tactical/Items.cc:3002`) writes the whole `gMercProfiles[ubProfile].uiMoney` into a slot with no clamping, bypassing the cap entirely:

```cpp
if ( usItem == MONEY ) CreateMoney( gMercProfiles[ ubProfile ].uiMoney, &(pSoldier->inv[ bLoop ] ) );
```

Currently latent rather than observed in play, so it is filed here for the same fix pass rather than as its own issue.

## Underlying design problem

The duality is the real defect: `uiMoney` is a scalar on the profile (`LoadSaveMercProfile.cc:213` persists it separately from `inv[]`), while a money stack is a size-capped object in a pocket. Any flow that crosses between the two — spawn, gift, profile write — can lose or corrupt money, and each crossing re-implements the split rules. `DropObjInSlot` underflows on the same assumption from the other direction.

Per the repo's native-first rule, the fix should not preserve the "one slot" shape: the head bounty should be paid as an **amount** credited to the player, letting the normal give-item path distribute it across pockets. That removes the `FindObj`-first-slot dependency entirely. The minimal legacy-shaped patch — gift *every* money slot rather than the first — treats the symptom and keeps the fragile assumption.

## Definition of done

- The player receives the full bounty for any number of heads: 3 heads pays $30,000, 4 pays $40,000. Asserted in a test, not by hand.
- A gtest stages the payout with 3 heads and asserts $30,000 reaches the player. Per `AGENTS.md` a mechanic that cannot be asserted headless is not done — it must be drivable without a click path or a screenshot.
- Any NPC `usGiftItem = MONEY` handover pays the **whole** amount held, not the first stack. If the gift path is kept at all, it iterates money slots rather than calling `FindObj` once.
- `NPC.cc:1811` no longer throws an uncaught `std::logic_error` on a missing gift item.
- The `DropObjInSlot` merge is correct when `pInSlot->uiMoneyAmount > MoneySlotLimit` — no unsigned underflow, leftover dropped or re-placed cleanly. Covered by a unit test.
- `PlaceObjectInSoldierProfile` clamps to `MoneySlotLimit` when writing `uiMoney` into a slot, so an over-cap stack cannot be created in the first place.

## Notes

- No gameplay rebalance: $10,000 per head is the intended value and stays.
- `python tools/dev.py test` before and after; the change is in the money path, so the existing item tests are the regression net.
- Track: **Revamp gameplay**. The bounty payout is a quest/economy correctness fix in the tactical NPC gift flow.
- Touches `src/game/Tactical/{Items,Soldier_Create}.cc` and `src/game/TacticalAI/NPC.cc`; no JSON or Lua changes, and `assets/externalized/` is untouched.

## Objective

Create a controlled integration path between the external contributor work by @eij0471 and MyZubster, without granting broad VPS access.

## Scope

The external component should remain independently reproducible and communicate with MyZubster through a constrained bridge/API.

Target flow:

external Docker node → Evidence Payload verifier → authenticated bridge/API → MyZubster evidence store → Knowledge Card / Contributor Passport → Knowledge Graph / pilot view

## Security requirements

- no direct unrestricted shell/VPS access;
- dedicated credential/token with minimum privileges;
- isolated service/container or sandbox path;
- no secrets committed to GitHub;
- explicit port/endpoints documentation;
- request/response schema documented;
- logging enabled;
- rate/permission boundaries documented;
- revoke/rotate credential procedure documented.

## Technical milestones

### 1. Reproducible local package
Contributor provides:
- repository/branch;
- Dockerfile / compose instructions;
- dependency versions;
- startup command;
- expected ports;
- health check;
- sample sanitized Evidence Payload;
- deterministic verifier output/logs.

### 2. Contract
Define the minimal integration contract:
- payload schema/version;
- digest/hash field;
- evidence state;
- contributor identity reference;
- pilot reference;
- response/verification status;
- error model.

### 3. Local interoperability test
Run the external node against a local or mock MyZubster bridge.

Acceptance:
- payload accepted/rejected deterministically;
- SHA-256/digest validated;
- malformed/unsupported payload rejected;
- logs preserved.

### 4. Controlled VPS test
Deploy only the isolated bridge/container to a constrained VPS environment.

Acceptance:
- dedicated token;
- no access outside assigned service;
- successful authenticated request;
- reproducible logs;
- rollback/revoke path tested.

### 5. MyZubster provenance
On successful review:
- link contribution to GitHub identity;
- create/update Knowledge Card;
- attach accepted PR/commit/test evidence;
- record status as TESTED or VERIFIED only when justified;
- expose the result in Contributor Passport / Knowledge Graph where supported.

## Related work

- contributor coordination: #1474
- N4K48 / Node Bridge direction
- MYZ bounty governance: #1502
- Open Period Care / Circular Care evidence path

## Definition of done

This issue is done when an independently reproducible external node can submit or verify a sanitized Evidence Payload through an authenticated, least-privilege MyZubster bridge and the accepted result can be linked to contributor provenance without exposing VPS secrets or granting unrestricted infrastructure access.

Related bounty: #61. Related QA scope: #57.

Both `AgentStream.toDataStreamResponse()` and `toTextStreamResponse()` consume the source in an async `ReadableStream.start()` loop. They ignore backpressure and have no cancel handler that closes the source iterator.

A finite source yields all 20 events before any response-body read. Cancelling with a pending source read does not forward cancellation to the iterator; late reads can attempt to write to a closed controller.

## Environment and reproduction

- main: `18b8aa4680bcc03ddb475a4ec00451bfe1540a89`
- package: `@vistagenic/vista@0.3.9`
- Windows, Node.js v24.16.0
- Tested against committed dist and rebuilt source.
- Surface: runtime `vista/ai` response adapter, applicable to server routes returning these responses. Reproduced directly in Node, without generate/dev/start/deploy integration.
- No API key or live model calls needed.

Save the following as `reproduce-vista-stream.cjs` and run against a clean checkout:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/Mantitup-Org/vista.git
cd vista
git checkout 18b8aa4680bcc03ddb475a4ec00451bfe1540a89
node /path/to/reproduce-vista-stream.cjs .
```

```js
const assert = require('node:assert/strict');
const path = require('node:path');
const { setImmediate: nextTurn } = require('node:timers/promises');
const root = path.resolve(process.argv[2] || '.');
const { AgentStream } = require(path.join(root, 'packages/vista/dist/ai/stream.js'));
(async () => {
  let failed = false;
  for (const method of ['toDataStreamResponse', 'toTextStreamResponse']) {
    let produced = 0;
    let cleanedUp = false;
    async function* source() {
      try {
        for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {
          produced++;
          yield { type: 'text-delta', textDelta: String(i) };
        }
      } finally { cleanedUp = true; }
    }
    const response = new AgentStream(source())[method]();
    await nextTurn();
    const prefetched = produced;
    await response.body.cancel('client disconnected');
    console.log(JSON.stringify({ method, prefetched, cleanedUp }));
    try { assert.equal(prefetched, 1); assert.equal(cleanedUp, true); }
    catch { failed = true; }
  }
  process.exitCode = failed ? 1 : 0;
})().catch(error => { console.error(error); process.exitCode = 1; });
```

Actual on original: both adapters log `prefetched: 20` before any body read; exit status 1. In this finite example, cleanup occurs through normal exhaustion; that is not evidence of cancellation propagation.

Expected: pause source reads when the output queue is full and close the iterator on cancellation. The local candidate logs `prefetched: 1`, `cleanedUp: true`; exit status 0.

The absent cancellation forwarding is also reproducible with a source iterator whose `next()` remains pending: calling `response.body.cancel()` never invokes its `return()`. The candidate's regression tests cover this and late resolution/rejection.

## Candidate and evidence

The local candidate uses demand-driven pull and a cancellation-aware iterator adapter. It includes matching rebuilt dist and documentation. The original 10-test suite failed 6 tests; the fixed suite adds two race tests and passes all 12. TypeScript package build, existing AI tests (14), test:agents, integrity checks (102), and diff whitespace checks pass.

Tests cover pending next() resolve/reject after cancellation, cancellation during async error cleanup, SSE/plaintext output, initialization and upstream errors.

Not exercised: real model API abort, browser/server disconnect integration, live deployment, full Rust suite. Iterator cleanup cannot guarantee immediate interruption of already-pending upstream operations; callers should pass an abortSignal when needed. This is a local response-adapter finding, not a claim about a deployed site or provider billing.

I reviewed the issue/PR lists and PR #92; its provider parsing changes do not address these response adapters. Please confirm whether this finding is useful and previously unreported.

Development and verification were AI-assisted with Codex. No upstream patch PR has been submitted. The local candidate is available after confirmation, following #61.

